WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:07.030
Hi, everyone.

00:00:07.030 --> 00:00:09.190
Welcome back.

00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:10.420
Lecture 2.

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:14.010
So today, we will talk
about prefix-free codes

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:18.090
and Kraft's inequality, which
we will learn about shortly.

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:22.770
Right, so let's start with
the quiz from last time, which

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:25.230
will also function as a
recap of the last lecture.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.360
OK, so we want to design
fixed-length codes

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:32.729
for an alphabet of size 9.

00:00:32.729 --> 00:00:35.670
And the first question was,
what is the length in bits

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:38.220
per symbol for such a code?

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.080
And in the last class,
we saw this formula

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:43.080
that if your alphabet
is like this x,

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:52.780
then the bits per symbol
is log base 2 the size

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:55.700
of the alphabet,
the ceiling of that.

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:58.960
So in simple words,
basically you

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:03.280
find the nearest power of two,
which is bigger than your size.

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:07.650
So 9 lies between 8 and 16.

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:08.910
So you take 16--

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:13.230
you take log base 2
of 16, which is 4.

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:15.660
And so 4 is the
number of bits you

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:17.160
need if you are
going to represent

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:22.100
this in a fixed-length code.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:23.453
Is Everybody OK with that?

00:01:23.453 --> 00:01:25.745
I think most of you got this
one right, so that's good.

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:31.580
OK, so one thing you
will immediately observe

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:34.190
is if your alphabet
has size 8, then

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:38.460
you're spending
log 8 equal to 3.

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:43.350
If it's 16, you're spending
log 16, which is 4.

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:46.500
So powers of 2, this seems
to be working really nicely.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:48.990
But for 9, you see clearly--

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:49.950
you want this, right?

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:51.090
This is what you want.

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:53.550
This is somewhere
between 3 and 4.

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:55.050
But this is not what you got.

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:58.830
You got 4, which just
seems suboptimal.

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:02.250
So this question was trying
to introduce you to one idea

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:04.320
to make it better.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.240
So the idea is simple.

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:08.370
Rather than encoding
one symbol at a time,

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:12.060
you encode symbols
in blocks of two.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:21.570
So if your alphabet was
like A, B, C, D, up to H, I,

00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:25.260
so the original code, what you
would do is A becomes 0000,

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:30.235
B becomes 0001, so on, up to I.

00:02:30.235 --> 00:02:31.860
Now what you will do
is you will create

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:38.080
a new code, which is like AA,
AB, AC, AD, dot, dot, dot,

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:38.920
up to II.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:43.520
So effectively,
what you're doing is

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:46.040
you're creating an
alphabet of size 81.

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:48.440
You're creating the product
alphabet of these two.

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:50.390
And then we asked what
is the length in bits

00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:53.090
per symbol for this code--

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:58.800
like a fixed-length code
applied on blocks of two.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:03.190
Here, there were
mistakes, both on our side

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:06.070
because we made a typo in
the next question, which

00:03:06.070 --> 00:03:08.650
led to some of the students
getting this wrong.

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:11.603
But I think you got
the concept so again,

00:03:11.603 --> 00:03:12.520
we use the same trick.

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:15.040
We know that 81 is less
than or equal to 128

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:16.720
and greater than 64.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:18.160
This is 2 power 7.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:19.780
This is 2 power 6.

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:25.690
So really, if you calculate
this, this will be 7.

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:28.540
So many of you got it
correct that you need

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:30.940
7 bits to represent this pair.

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:33.160
So AA becomes like 7 bits.

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:36.640
So this becomes 0000000.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:40.150
And then so on for
the other guys.

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:42.370
However, the question we
asked was bits per symbol,

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:43.810
not bits per block.

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:46.210
So here, the bits
per symbol is--

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:48.430
each block has two symbols.

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:53.710
So just to make it very
clear, block is like AA,

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:57.550
and symbol is like A--

00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:00.920
so each symbol and then
you have a block of two.

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:03.020
So the length in
bits per symbol is

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:04.850
this divided by 2,
which is this divided

00:04:04.850 --> 00:04:09.920
by 2, which is 3.5 here.

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:12.890
And those who wrote seven,
we will still give you marks

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:17.029
because we made a
mistake in the next part.

00:04:17.029 --> 00:04:18.410
So let me read the question.

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:21.260
So now the alphabet size is
81 because we will be encoding

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:22.760
pairs of symbols together.

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:26.460
So the length in bits per--
so this was the typo we made;

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:27.980
It should be bits per block--

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:30.380
is log 81 7.

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:32.540
However, we are encoding
blocks of two symbols,

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:36.950
so the length in bits per
symbol is this divided by 2 3.5.

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:40.040
And did working in blocks of
two give a better compression

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:41.240
ratio?

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:43.430
And I think most of
you got it right.

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:45.140
The answer is yes.

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:47.360
So we went from
4 bits per base--

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:50.390
4 bits per symbol when we were
encoding them one at a time

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:53.740
versus like 3.5.

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:54.910
I wanted to do this--

00:04:54.910 --> 00:05:00.190
can someone be kind and tell
me what is log 9 base 2?

00:05:00.190 --> 00:05:02.260
I just want to see how
close we got to that.

00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:07.590
It's like 3.17.

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:08.430
OK.

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:10.650
Yeah, 9 is very
close to 8, right?

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:15.240
So we assume it's only a little
bit above log 8, which is 3.

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:16.930
So we got to 3.5.

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:19.230
We didn't quite get to log 9.

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:21.870
Can somebody suggest how
I can get even closer

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:24.705
to this many bits per symbol?

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:28.880
Larger blocks?

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:30.050
Yeah, very good.

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:32.960
The answer was larger
blocks, simple idea.

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:35.280
Instead of doing two at a
time, you do three at a time.

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:38.542
So you have nine cubed
possibilities and so on.

00:05:38.542 --> 00:05:39.500
And you can keep going.

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:44.030
And you can show
actually if you just

00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:45.980
do some math that you
will actually eventually

00:05:45.980 --> 00:05:49.410
converge to 3.17.

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:52.800
And we will see this sort of
scheme and even better ideas

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:55.260
to achieve this because you
can see that this is not

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:57.360
particularly optimal.

00:05:57.360 --> 00:05:59.702
Your alphabet is just
growing exponentially

00:05:59.702 --> 00:06:01.410
as you make your block
bigger and bigger.

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:02.850
So we will come back to this.

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:05.220
For now, I guess
just notice that you

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:06.870
can do better than
fixed-length codes

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:09.030
by using some clever ideas.

00:06:09.030 --> 00:06:14.770
OK, any questions on
this first question?

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:15.270
OK.

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:20.225
Second one is very simple.

00:06:20.225 --> 00:06:21.600
This is the sort
of quiz question

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:24.840
like you listen in class and
you solve it like in a second

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:25.620
basically.

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:30.810
Yeah, I think everybody
has got this right,

00:06:30.810 --> 00:06:32.730
but let me quickly do it.

00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:34.890
So the length-- so
the question is,

00:06:34.890 --> 00:06:37.890
you are given a
random sequence, which

00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:41.160
is sampled from some
probability distribution,

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:44.430
and you encode it
with this code.

00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:47.040
You want to compute
the expected code

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:50.850
length, which is our usual
metric for determining

00:06:50.850 --> 00:06:52.260
the goodness of a code.

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:56.850
So the length of each code word,
you can just write 1, 2, 2.

00:06:56.850 --> 00:06:59.340
And then you can
calculate the expectation.

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:02.850
This is just making sure you
remember your probability

00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:07.710
so PA times length
of A plus PB times

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:13.530
length of B plus PC
times length of C,

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:21.135
which is 1 plus 0.3 times 2.

00:07:28.990 --> 00:07:33.040
OK, 1.5-- I hope
I got it right--

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:35.200
bits per symbol.

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:37.930
I find it useful to always
write the unit bits per symbol

00:07:37.930 --> 00:07:40.000
because that way you
don't make mistakes,

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:45.670
like the mistake that
we made in the question.

00:07:45.670 --> 00:07:47.065
OK, any questions on this?

00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:53.550
OK, good.

00:07:53.550 --> 00:07:57.750
OK, I gave an exercise in the
last class to decode this.

00:07:57.750 --> 00:07:59.265
Was anybody able to decode?

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:04.040
So this was the code we gave.

00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:07.670
And I wrote down a
encoded sequence,

00:08:07.670 --> 00:08:13.760
and I wanted you to get back
to the original sequence.

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:14.900
I see a couple of hands.

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:15.650
Anybody else?

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:21.020
OK, let me ask
you for the first,

00:08:21.020 --> 00:08:24.340
and then I will ask
you for the second.

00:08:24.340 --> 00:08:27.323
So what's the first symbol,
first recorded symbol?

00:08:27.323 --> 00:08:28.190
D.

00:08:28.190 --> 00:08:32.659
So the answer given
is D. And why is it D?

00:08:32.659 --> 00:08:35.960
Because the only column
that starts with a 1

00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:38.400
and then has another one
and has another one is D.

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:38.900
Right.

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:41.690
And the answer is
that-- the reason why--

00:08:41.690 --> 00:08:43.580
it can only be D,
right, because you have

00:08:43.580 --> 00:08:47.490
a code word which is like 111.

00:08:47.490 --> 00:08:49.560
If it was A, then it
must have a 0 somewhere.

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:51.540
And if it is B,
then one 0, so on.

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:53.580
So there is only one
possibility really.

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:56.100
And we'll come back to
this later in the lecture.

00:08:56.100 --> 00:08:57.390
The next one.

00:08:57.390 --> 00:08:59.700
Yeah, so the next
letter would be C.

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:02.430
And then we basically can
just keep reading each letter

00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:03.780
until we get to a 0.

00:09:08.170 --> 00:09:10.160
That's a good way of thinking.

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:13.270
So the answer was
you just keep going

00:09:13.270 --> 00:09:15.820
until you hit a 0 because
of the particular way

00:09:15.820 --> 00:09:17.110
this code is structured.

00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:18.730
When you hit a 0--

00:09:18.730 --> 00:09:20.860
if you have three 1's
in a row without a 0,

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:22.990
then you know it's a
D. If you have two 1's

00:09:22.990 --> 00:09:26.920
and then a 0, then you
know it's a C, and so on.

00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:28.600
Let me decode a couple now.

00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:31.810
So after this, you have 1.

00:09:31.810 --> 00:09:35.710
So when you see a 1, you
know it's either B, C, or D.

00:09:35.710 --> 00:09:38.290
It can't be A, so
that's eliminated.

00:09:38.290 --> 00:09:40.510
And then you see a 0.

00:09:40.510 --> 00:09:42.640
So you immediately
know it has to be

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:45.820
a B. Because if it was a
C, then it will have a 11,

00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:47.230
and D also will have a 11.

00:09:47.230 --> 00:09:50.865
So it has to be a B. Sorry.

00:09:53.460 --> 00:09:57.150
And then you see a 0, and you
know only one code word starts

00:09:57.150 --> 00:10:02.070
with a 0, which is
an A, and so on.

00:10:02.070 --> 00:10:04.200
I hope you are
decoding this at home.

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:07.440
But I hope you get the
gist that even if you

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:08.670
have a variable length code--

00:10:08.670 --> 00:10:10.378
this was a variable
length code, and last

00:10:10.378 --> 00:10:13.200
lecture we had some concerns
from students that how will

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:13.950
you decode it.

00:10:13.950 --> 00:10:16.950
But you can see there
are variable length

00:10:16.950 --> 00:10:18.210
codes that can be decoded.

00:10:18.210 --> 00:10:19.560
And this is one example.

00:10:19.560 --> 00:10:21.960
And we'll look at
it in more detail

00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:27.640
and characterize when exactly
can you decode these things.

00:10:27.640 --> 00:10:30.310
Any questions on this?

00:10:30.310 --> 00:10:31.990
Anybody who wasn't
able to decode

00:10:31.990 --> 00:10:36.140
and doesn't fully understand
the process right now?

00:10:39.060 --> 00:10:39.720
OK.

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:42.300
And we will go through an
actual algorithm for those

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:44.845
who like to think in that way.

00:10:44.845 --> 00:10:45.345
Right.

00:10:48.920 --> 00:10:51.470
OK, so the outline for today--

00:10:51.470 --> 00:10:53.517
no sense going through
the list like you will--

00:10:53.517 --> 00:10:55.100
we'll get to them
when we get to them.

00:10:55.100 --> 00:10:56.870
But the first item
is we will define

00:10:56.870 --> 00:11:00.470
what do we mean by lossless
compression in this context.

00:11:00.470 --> 00:11:04.085
And we will discuss two
specific categories of codes.

00:11:18.810 --> 00:11:20.835
OK, so I will make a--

00:11:20.835 --> 00:11:23.280
I will write down
a few codes, and I

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:25.305
will ask you whether
they are lossless or not.

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:35.452
OK, is this lossless?

00:11:35.452 --> 00:11:37.240
No.

00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:38.620
I hear no.

00:11:38.620 --> 00:11:39.880
Why is it not lossless?

00:11:44.475 --> 00:11:46.378
Because of the ambiguity
of the symbols.

00:11:46.378 --> 00:11:48.170
Yeah, because of the
ambiguity in the sense

00:11:48.170 --> 00:11:50.600
that A and B have the same
encoding, so clearly--

00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:52.430
if you see a 0--

00:11:52.430 --> 00:11:54.470
you only see 0's,
so you can never

00:11:54.470 --> 00:11:56.750
tell whether A or B
was transmitted, right?

00:11:56.750 --> 00:11:58.070
So this is not lossless.

00:11:58.070 --> 00:12:01.790
This is very much not lossless.

00:12:01.790 --> 00:12:02.990
OK, now this one.

00:12:09.160 --> 00:12:10.030
Is this lossless?

00:12:17.254 --> 00:12:20.740
It's the same as the other one.

00:12:20.740 --> 00:12:22.480
So is it lossless or not?

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:23.340
It is.

00:12:23.340 --> 00:12:24.440
I'm sorry, no, it's not.

00:12:24.440 --> 00:12:25.190
It's not lossless.

00:12:25.190 --> 00:12:29.150
Anybody else who has
a differing opinion?

00:12:29.150 --> 00:12:31.280
What do we mean by
lossless, I guess, right?

00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:34.700
If you know you're only going
to ever send one symbol,

00:12:34.700 --> 00:12:36.680
then this is lossless, right?

00:12:36.680 --> 00:12:38.570
If you see one 0,
then it's an A.

00:12:38.570 --> 00:12:41.870
If you see two 0's, then
it's a B. So in that sense,

00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:42.500
it is lossless.

00:12:42.500 --> 00:12:46.430
But very rarely do you want
to send only one symbol.

00:12:46.430 --> 00:12:49.850
Most often, you want to
send a sequence of symbols.

00:12:49.850 --> 00:12:55.940
And in that context, if you
see that AA will encode to 00,

00:12:55.940 --> 00:12:57.680
B will also encode to 00.

00:12:57.680 --> 00:13:02.210
So if you receive a 00, you
don't know whether the original

00:13:02.210 --> 00:13:06.740
person sent a AA or a B. So in
that sense, it's not lossless.

00:13:09.920 --> 00:13:12.860
But you already start seeing
that the definition of lossless

00:13:12.860 --> 00:13:15.350
is not obvious.

00:13:15.350 --> 00:13:17.030
You need to define it carefully.

00:13:17.030 --> 00:13:19.010
Otherwise, it's
not clear what do

00:13:19.010 --> 00:13:22.220
you mean when you say
it's a lossless code.

00:13:22.220 --> 00:13:23.690
So let's define it.

00:13:43.780 --> 00:13:46.330
OK, so we will define
something called

00:13:46.330 --> 00:13:53.490
a uniquely decodable code.

00:13:53.490 --> 00:13:55.740
And it's a very
simple definition.

00:13:58.890 --> 00:14:22.870
So a code is uniquely decodable
if no two input sequences,

00:14:22.870 --> 00:14:35.400
say xn, ym, where m and n are
greater than or equal to 1,

00:14:35.400 --> 00:14:46.480
are encoded to the same code.

00:14:46.480 --> 00:14:51.325
Or I guess let's
call it same output.

00:14:57.080 --> 00:14:58.580
Are we happy with
that definition?

00:14:58.580 --> 00:15:00.320
Any questions on
this definition?

00:15:18.070 --> 00:15:21.880
Right, so with this
definition, if we go back--

00:15:21.880 --> 00:15:22.990
let me draw that again.

00:15:26.640 --> 00:15:30.710
So like this code that we
saw, A was 0, B was 00,

00:15:30.710 --> 00:15:33.140
is not uniquely decodable.

00:15:46.710 --> 00:15:47.865
Before we move on--

00:15:53.100 --> 00:15:56.460
so when we talk, for example,
we have a sequence of words.

00:15:56.460 --> 00:15:59.100
And sometimes, you
have two words such

00:15:59.100 --> 00:16:01.830
that they are individually
words, but when you join them,

00:16:01.830 --> 00:16:02.820
that's another word.

00:16:02.820 --> 00:16:06.430
That's possible in
English, for example.

00:16:06.430 --> 00:16:09.240
So when you talk and
you talk to someone,

00:16:09.240 --> 00:16:13.347
how does the other person
know when does the word end

00:16:13.347 --> 00:16:14.805
and when does the
other word start?

00:16:17.430 --> 00:16:22.480
Forget about codes for a
second, just very generally.

00:16:22.480 --> 00:16:23.760
We have spaces.

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:25.410
You have spaces.

00:16:25.410 --> 00:16:25.920
Very good.

00:16:25.920 --> 00:16:26.370
Very good.

00:16:26.370 --> 00:16:26.870
Right.

00:16:26.870 --> 00:16:30.990
So oftentimes-- this is one
way of thinking about it

00:16:30.990 --> 00:16:33.570
because the way we are thinking
about it is when you have AA,

00:16:33.570 --> 00:16:36.540
AA is encoded to
00 without any gap.

00:16:36.540 --> 00:16:37.950
It's just 0 followed by 0.

00:16:37.950 --> 00:16:39.090
There isn't a gap.

00:16:39.090 --> 00:16:42.450
B is also 00, no gaps anywhere.

00:16:42.450 --> 00:16:45.270
So that's the sort
of setting where

00:16:45.270 --> 00:16:48.090
we are thinking about these
uniquely decodable codes.

00:16:48.090 --> 00:16:51.750
And most often when you are
working in any real application

00:16:51.750 --> 00:16:55.320
today, with computers
and so on, you

00:16:55.320 --> 00:16:57.547
can't afford gaps, because
you have a binary alphabet

00:16:57.547 --> 00:16:58.380
you're working with.

00:16:58.380 --> 00:16:59.730
You have 0 and you have 1.

00:16:59.730 --> 00:17:04.020
You don't have a third symbol to
signify that now one code has--

00:17:04.020 --> 00:17:08.220
one code word has ended,
now we will pause for a sec.

00:17:08.220 --> 00:17:12.900
In the old days,
some of you might

00:17:12.900 --> 00:17:14.533
have heard of the Morse code.

00:17:14.533 --> 00:17:16.200
So this was used in
the telegraph, which

00:17:16.200 --> 00:17:20.130
was the only mode of
intercontinental communication

00:17:20.130 --> 00:17:22.619
for a long period.

00:17:22.619 --> 00:17:25.290
And even during my dad's
time, that was the way

00:17:25.290 --> 00:17:30.450
people sent fast
messages in India.

00:17:30.450 --> 00:17:34.380
So Morse code is comprised
of dots and dashes.

00:17:34.380 --> 00:17:37.110
So it's a way to-- you
can represent any letter

00:17:37.110 --> 00:17:39.090
or any number in this code.

00:17:39.090 --> 00:17:44.360
And you have a dot,
or you have a dash.

00:17:44.360 --> 00:17:47.090
So let's look at the code,
and then we'll come back.

00:17:47.090 --> 00:17:48.440
You can see this, right?

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:49.970
So for example, E is a dot.

00:17:52.510 --> 00:17:55.070
S is a dot dot dot.

00:17:55.070 --> 00:17:58.310
Q is a dash dash dot dash.

00:17:58.310 --> 00:18:00.320
So you just send a
sequence of these.

00:18:00.320 --> 00:18:03.620
And on the other side,
just listening to dot dash,

00:18:03.620 --> 00:18:05.840
the other person can decode it.

00:18:05.840 --> 00:18:07.820
So this was the Morse code.

00:18:07.820 --> 00:18:11.030
And how did the Morse code
denote the separation?

00:18:11.030 --> 00:18:13.490
So I guess if you look at
this, you will immediately

00:18:13.490 --> 00:18:19.560
see that you have E, you
have A, and you have T,

00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:21.600
if you look at these three.

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:25.680
So a E followed by a T is
a dot followed by a dash.

00:18:25.680 --> 00:18:30.240
But an A is also a dot
followed by a dash.

00:18:30.240 --> 00:18:33.627
So Morse code, in our sense,
is not uniquely decodable.

00:18:33.627 --> 00:18:35.460
So this is not uniquely
decodable in the way

00:18:35.460 --> 00:18:36.870
we think about
it, but clearly it

00:18:36.870 --> 00:18:39.210
was used for communication
for a long period.

00:18:39.210 --> 00:18:40.110
And why is that?

00:18:40.110 --> 00:18:45.030
Because between letters,
you would just put a space.

00:18:45.030 --> 00:18:46.318
You will pause.

00:18:46.318 --> 00:18:47.860
You will not send
anything for a bit.

00:18:47.860 --> 00:18:51.092
And then you will start
sending the next letter.

00:18:51.092 --> 00:18:52.800
And then the receiver
would know how to--

00:18:52.800 --> 00:18:55.290
I guess initially some
human used to do it,

00:18:55.290 --> 00:18:58.230
and then they made
machines that do it.

00:18:58.230 --> 00:19:00.510
But the idea is that
in different types

00:19:00.510 --> 00:19:02.280
of communication
systems, you can have

00:19:02.280 --> 00:19:04.560
different ways of doing things.

00:19:04.560 --> 00:19:06.990
Like, for us, this
is very important.

00:19:06.990 --> 00:19:08.490
This is what we'll
be working with.

00:19:08.490 --> 00:19:12.390
And this is how most modern
compression algorithms work.

00:19:12.390 --> 00:19:16.650
But there are other systems
also, so you should be aware--

00:19:16.650 --> 00:19:19.050
putting a comma after
every letter, in a way.

00:19:23.320 --> 00:19:25.660
Anything interesting
somebody observes?

00:19:25.660 --> 00:19:28.000
Like, anything that we saw
in the last class and you see

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:28.960
reflected here?

00:19:28.960 --> 00:19:31.450
Look at the lengths of
the different code words.

00:19:31.450 --> 00:19:35.380
Anybody wants to--
what do you see here?

00:19:35.380 --> 00:19:37.810
Anything interesting
with the letters

00:19:37.810 --> 00:19:40.090
and which letters are
getting shorter codes

00:19:40.090 --> 00:19:43.004
and which letters are
getting longer codes?

00:19:43.004 --> 00:19:44.120
Go ahead.

00:19:44.120 --> 00:19:47.840
The vowels are the shortest.

00:19:47.840 --> 00:19:51.520
Yeah, so the vowels are shorter.

00:19:51.520 --> 00:19:52.990
That's one suggestion.

00:19:52.990 --> 00:19:54.520
And that's true.

00:19:54.520 --> 00:19:55.420
E is a single dot.

00:19:55.420 --> 00:19:57.730
I is a dot dot.

00:19:57.730 --> 00:19:59.290
Some of the vowels
are a bit longer,

00:19:59.290 --> 00:20:02.110
like U is a dot dot dash.

00:20:02.110 --> 00:20:04.930
But you see T is very short.

00:20:04.930 --> 00:20:07.390
And if you study the frequency
of letters in English,

00:20:07.390 --> 00:20:10.990
you will see that E and T
are the two most commonly

00:20:10.990 --> 00:20:13.150
used letters.

00:20:13.150 --> 00:20:16.870
So the vowels are shorter
because they are also usually

00:20:16.870 --> 00:20:19.090
very commonly used.

00:20:19.090 --> 00:20:21.820
So even in the Morse
code, in those old days,

00:20:21.820 --> 00:20:24.760
they knew that for more
frequently used letters,

00:20:24.760 --> 00:20:28.450
you should use a shorter symbol
so that your overall message

00:20:28.450 --> 00:20:30.610
becomes shorter.

00:20:30.610 --> 00:20:34.195
So that's just, I think,
an interesting fact.

00:20:37.940 --> 00:20:39.020
Any questions with this?

00:20:43.600 --> 00:20:45.390
Could you go back to
the previous slide?

00:20:45.390 --> 00:20:45.890
This one?

00:20:48.640 --> 00:20:53.980
Yeah, so the x and then ym--

00:20:53.980 --> 00:20:56.020
So there are just
two input sequences.

00:20:56.020 --> 00:21:03.250
So if we were to give an
example, here, if you take x2,

00:21:03.250 --> 00:21:12.770
which is like a two tuple sort
of, x1, x2, which is like A, A.

00:21:12.770 --> 00:21:16.370
Or you do y-- or you take y1,
which is just a single input,

00:21:16.370 --> 00:21:22.460
which is B, then you can see
that the encoding of x2 is

00:21:22.460 --> 00:21:28.460
the same as the encoding
of y1, which is 00.

00:21:28.460 --> 00:21:30.290
So that's what we mean.

00:21:30.290 --> 00:21:31.770
Sorry if the notation is new.

00:21:31.770 --> 00:21:37.050
I think I will define it more
properly in the next part.

00:21:37.050 --> 00:21:38.720
But you will also
get used to it.

00:21:38.720 --> 00:21:41.450
But yeah, that's just
a tuple, x2 or xn.

00:21:41.450 --> 00:21:45.170
So we are saying that it's
not like n and m don't

00:21:45.170 --> 00:21:46.340
need to be the same.

00:21:46.340 --> 00:21:48.770
Any two sequences with
different lengths,

00:21:48.770 --> 00:21:51.525
they should never map
to the same output.

00:21:51.525 --> 00:21:52.940
They should be unique.

00:21:52.940 --> 00:21:56.540
Given the output, you
are not able to decode.

00:21:56.540 --> 00:21:59.180
You also know how many
input symbols are there.

00:21:59.180 --> 00:22:01.520
So it's a very strict
property in that.

00:22:04.600 --> 00:22:06.200
Is that clear?

00:22:06.200 --> 00:22:08.720
OK.

00:22:08.720 --> 00:22:12.440
So we will not talk about
uniquely decodable codes

00:22:12.440 --> 00:22:14.570
for the rest of the class.

00:22:14.570 --> 00:22:16.430
You will see a question
in the homework.

00:22:16.430 --> 00:22:19.550
And I will explain later why
we don't talk about these.

00:22:19.550 --> 00:22:22.610
It's a very powerful
property, but you don't really

00:22:22.610 --> 00:22:24.680
need this very
powerful property.

00:22:24.680 --> 00:22:27.680
We will see a much smaller class
of codes, which is very, very

00:22:27.680 --> 00:22:28.430
easy to deal with.

00:22:28.430 --> 00:22:29.710
And we'll just stick to those.

00:22:29.710 --> 00:22:31.040
So that's our next topic.

00:22:35.157 --> 00:22:36.990
Yeah, but there will
be a homework question,

00:22:36.990 --> 00:22:39.825
where you will look at these
and do some proofs with these.

00:22:49.480 --> 00:22:52.135
Let me find the old code we had.

00:24:28.970 --> 00:24:34.340
So this is a proposed decoding
algorithm for this code.

00:24:34.340 --> 00:24:36.620
Let me talk through
this in words.

00:24:36.620 --> 00:24:40.700
So you start with an empty--

00:24:40.700 --> 00:24:42.620
you have a variable
called C, which

00:24:42.620 --> 00:24:47.840
is like storing your
code, like the encoding.

00:24:47.840 --> 00:24:49.550
You start with empty.

00:24:49.550 --> 00:24:51.110
You look at the
next bit you added

00:24:51.110 --> 00:24:53.900
to C. You check if
C is in the table,

00:24:53.900 --> 00:24:56.660
so if C is in one of
these four entries.

00:24:56.660 --> 00:25:01.700
If C is there, you decode, and
then you set C again to empty.

00:25:01.700 --> 00:25:03.590
And if C is not in
the table, you just

00:25:03.590 --> 00:25:05.480
continue and then
look for the next bit,

00:25:05.480 --> 00:25:07.430
and you keep adding the bit.

00:25:07.430 --> 00:25:10.490
So very simple algorithm,
linear time and so on.

00:25:14.260 --> 00:25:15.740
I will do an example.

00:25:15.740 --> 00:25:20.720
But before that, do people
generally understand

00:25:20.720 --> 00:25:23.330
the idea behind the thing?

00:25:23.330 --> 00:25:24.800
OK, let me do an example.

00:25:24.800 --> 00:25:26.270
I think that should help.

00:25:31.590 --> 00:25:34.530
And as Pulkit said, apologies
if you have done information

00:25:34.530 --> 00:25:36.615
theory, all this you know.

00:25:36.615 --> 00:25:41.100
I think probably this
lecture and the next lecture

00:25:41.100 --> 00:25:43.740
will be mostly recap for
the info theory experts.

00:25:43.740 --> 00:25:46.710
But after that, you will
see a lot of new material.

00:25:49.930 --> 00:25:52.810
OK, so let's do an
example for the same code.

00:26:02.580 --> 00:26:08.280
So we have let's say 011000--

00:26:08.280 --> 00:26:09.120
something.

00:26:12.860 --> 00:26:14.750
So we do it step by step.

00:26:14.750 --> 00:26:16.670
First step, C is equal to 0.

00:26:16.670 --> 00:26:20.880
Sorry, first step, C
is nothing, so null.

00:26:20.880 --> 00:26:22.860
Then C is 0.

00:26:22.860 --> 00:26:27.670
You decode A because
you immediately see

00:26:27.670 --> 00:26:29.680
that 0 is in this table here.

00:26:29.680 --> 00:26:31.900
So you decode.

00:26:31.900 --> 00:26:34.480
So this is your code word
you're trying to decode.

00:26:34.480 --> 00:26:36.760
Then C again becomes empty.

00:26:36.760 --> 00:26:38.710
Then you put C equal to 1.

00:26:38.710 --> 00:26:40.390
You can't decode here.

00:26:40.390 --> 00:26:42.820
There is no code
word which is 1.

00:26:42.820 --> 00:26:44.590
Then you see C is 11.

00:26:44.590 --> 00:26:47.260
Again, there is no
code word which is 11.

00:26:47.260 --> 00:26:49.600
Then you see C is 110.

00:26:49.600 --> 00:26:55.420
And here you can decode C

00:26:55.420 --> 00:26:58.540
OK, can somebody help me
with the next few steps?

00:26:58.540 --> 00:27:01.420
What happens next in this?

00:27:01.420 --> 00:27:04.585
So after I decode C, what is
the value of my variable C?

00:27:07.440 --> 00:27:09.410
Yeah, C becomes empty again.

00:27:09.410 --> 00:27:12.390
Right after you decode,
you reset C. OK, next step.

00:27:17.060 --> 00:27:19.000
So we are here, basically.

00:27:19.000 --> 00:27:20.874
Next symbol is this one.

00:27:20.874 --> 00:27:22.490
Add 0.

00:27:22.490 --> 00:27:25.510
Yeah, so next step you
insert 0 in C, right?

00:27:25.510 --> 00:27:27.070
Are we ready to decode?

00:27:27.070 --> 00:27:28.390
Yeah.

00:27:28.390 --> 00:27:28.960
Yeah.

00:27:28.960 --> 00:27:31.560
Why are we ready to decode?

00:27:31.560 --> 00:27:33.660
Because 0 is one of
the code words, right?

00:27:33.660 --> 00:27:36.150
0 is the encoding of
A. So you immediately

00:27:36.150 --> 00:27:40.680
decode A. And so on.

00:27:40.680 --> 00:27:41.910
Let's not do it too much.

00:27:46.900 --> 00:27:48.340
So let's think about this.

00:27:48.340 --> 00:27:50.440
First of all, any
confusion with this?

00:27:50.440 --> 00:27:52.165
Are we happy with the decoding?

00:27:56.340 --> 00:27:57.570
Why do we think this works?

00:27:57.570 --> 00:28:04.680
What is the property of the
code that makes this possible?

00:28:04.680 --> 00:28:08.970
Basically, no code is like
the prefix at the beginning

00:28:08.970 --> 00:28:11.040
of any other code.

00:28:11.040 --> 00:28:12.810
Yeah, so the answer
given by a student,

00:28:12.810 --> 00:28:15.990
which is the correct answer,
is that no code word is

00:28:15.990 --> 00:28:18.240
a prefix of another code word.

00:28:18.240 --> 00:28:22.680
So you see 0 is not a
prefix of any of these.

00:28:22.680 --> 00:28:24.990
So as soon as you see
a 0, you immediately

00:28:24.990 --> 00:28:27.510
know that it has to be A
because there is no other code

00:28:27.510 --> 00:28:30.570
word that starts with a 0.

00:28:30.570 --> 00:28:35.070
B, 10-- 10 is not a prefix
of any of the other guys.

00:28:35.070 --> 00:28:39.120
So as soon as you see a 10, it
has to be a B. It can't be C;

00:28:39.120 --> 00:28:44.070
it can't be D; it can't be A.
And so on for the other ones.

00:28:47.115 --> 00:28:49.100
So let me define it.

00:28:55.760 --> 00:28:58.370
So there are a few
different names

00:28:58.370 --> 00:29:00.380
for some historical
reasons, I assume.

00:29:00.380 --> 00:29:01.820
So they are called prefix codes.

00:29:01.820 --> 00:29:04.670
They are called
prefix-free codes.

00:29:07.280 --> 00:29:10.775
They are also called
instantaneous codes.

00:29:18.390 --> 00:29:20.680
OK, instantaneous codes.

00:29:25.610 --> 00:29:41.675
So no code word is a prefix
of another code word.

00:29:47.030 --> 00:29:49.145
Stop me if you don't follow.

00:29:53.270 --> 00:29:54.830
Why are they called
instantaneous?

00:29:54.830 --> 00:29:57.230
We just saw that property.

00:29:57.230 --> 00:29:59.960
As we were trying to decode,
as soon as we saw the symbol,

00:29:59.960 --> 00:30:02.990
we were able to
decode it instantly.

00:30:02.990 --> 00:30:04.850
We saw 0, we decoded it.

00:30:04.850 --> 00:30:08.840
We saw 110, we decoded a C.
You don't need to look ahead.

00:30:08.840 --> 00:30:11.503
You can do it in a more
streaming way as your symbols--

00:30:11.503 --> 00:30:12.920
as your bits are
coming in, you're

00:30:12.920 --> 00:30:14.330
immediately able to decode.

00:30:14.330 --> 00:30:18.890
That's why it's called
instantaneous code.

00:30:18.890 --> 00:30:20.600
Why is it called
prefix-free code?

00:30:20.600 --> 00:30:22.100
Just the definition.

00:30:22.100 --> 00:30:24.740
And why is it
called prefix codes?

00:30:24.740 --> 00:30:27.200
I think just people didn't
want to write prefix-free

00:30:27.200 --> 00:30:27.770
all the time.

00:30:32.620 --> 00:30:37.510
OK, so just a simple property--

00:30:41.620 --> 00:30:52.330
prefix-free codes are
uniquely decodable.

00:30:52.330 --> 00:30:54.670
So the property we saw
earlier, the uniquely decodable

00:30:54.670 --> 00:30:59.950
property, that no two
sequences map to the same code,

00:30:59.950 --> 00:31:03.490
prefix-free codes
satisfy that condition.

00:31:03.490 --> 00:31:06.220
Can somebody intuitively
describe why?

00:31:06.220 --> 00:31:08.200
We won't do a formal
proof of this.

00:31:08.200 --> 00:31:10.120
But just intuitively,
can somebody

00:31:10.120 --> 00:31:13.630
suggest why are prefix-free
codes uniquely decodable?

00:31:13.630 --> 00:31:14.660
Why are they lossless?

00:31:26.840 --> 00:31:28.520
When you go through
the algorithm

00:31:28.520 --> 00:31:32.750
and you find C in the table,
you are 100% guaranteed

00:31:32.750 --> 00:31:38.510
that it's not just a part
of another code word.

00:31:38.510 --> 00:31:43.820
It's that it is going to
be exactly C or exactly

00:31:43.820 --> 00:31:45.080
whatever you find.

00:31:45.080 --> 00:31:45.580
Yep.

00:31:45.580 --> 00:31:46.310
Yep, very good.

00:31:46.310 --> 00:31:50.030
Yeah, so the answer was as
you are doing the decoding

00:31:50.030 --> 00:31:54.020
and you decode 110 to
C, it has to be C. There

00:31:54.020 --> 00:31:56.300
is no other option really.

00:31:56.300 --> 00:31:58.880
So in a way, the
decoding algorithm

00:31:58.880 --> 00:32:02.005
proves that it's
decodable uniquely.

00:32:02.005 --> 00:32:03.380
There is that
decoding algorithm.

00:32:03.380 --> 00:32:06.250
And if you are--

00:32:06.250 --> 00:32:08.620
if you have done math, you
will want to be more formal,

00:32:08.620 --> 00:32:10.810
and we will leave you
to read the textbook

00:32:10.810 --> 00:32:12.400
if you want very formal proofs.

00:32:12.400 --> 00:32:14.440
But it's intuitively correct.

00:32:14.440 --> 00:32:17.845
And you just write that in math,
and you get the actual proof.

00:32:17.845 --> 00:32:19.720
But they are uniquely
decodable because there

00:32:19.720 --> 00:32:22.093
is a decoding algorithm,
which there is no confusion.

00:32:22.093 --> 00:32:24.010
You start from the
beginning, and you can just

00:32:24.010 --> 00:32:24.910
decode the sequence.

00:32:24.910 --> 00:32:26.350
There are no options really.

00:32:29.170 --> 00:32:31.510
Do we think the
opposite is also true?

00:32:31.510 --> 00:32:35.490
Let me not dwell on that
too much, but it is not.

00:32:35.490 --> 00:32:45.080
So uniquely decodable does
not imply it is prefix-free.

00:32:45.080 --> 00:32:47.330
So there are uniquely
decodable codes

00:32:47.330 --> 00:32:49.940
which are not prefix-free.

00:32:49.940 --> 00:32:54.440
And you will see
examples in homework.

00:32:54.440 --> 00:32:56.540
But again, we will see
that prefix-free codes

00:32:56.540 --> 00:32:59.180
are good enough for anything
we want to do really.

00:32:59.180 --> 00:33:02.512
So we will not study
uniquely decode.

00:33:02.512 --> 00:33:04.970
We will not study codes that
are uniquely decodable but not

00:33:04.970 --> 00:33:05.570
prefix-free.

00:33:05.570 --> 00:33:08.690
That's a category we will
just not worry about too much.

00:33:14.290 --> 00:33:18.510
OK, any questions about this?

00:33:18.510 --> 00:33:22.440
We will talk about the prefix
codes throughout the lecture.

00:33:22.440 --> 00:33:25.110
Hopefully by the end of it,
you will feel very comfortable

00:33:25.110 --> 00:33:25.710
with them.

00:33:29.500 --> 00:33:33.400
OK, so second topic
in the outline.

00:33:33.400 --> 00:33:36.580
So we saw one algorithm to
decode prefix-free codes

00:33:36.580 --> 00:33:39.040
already, somewhere here.

00:33:39.040 --> 00:33:41.740
You start with
something, and then you

00:33:41.740 --> 00:33:43.930
keep adding bits until
you find something.

00:33:43.930 --> 00:33:46.030
This is one algorithm.

00:33:46.030 --> 00:33:47.740
We will see another algorithm.

00:33:53.460 --> 00:33:57.240
How many of you have done
a algorithms class or data

00:33:57.240 --> 00:33:59.240
structures, seen trees?

00:34:02.580 --> 00:34:04.590
OK, a few of you
have seen trees.

00:34:04.590 --> 00:34:07.170
OK, even if you have not,
you will see trees now.

00:34:07.170 --> 00:34:08.580
So you will learn something.

00:34:08.580 --> 00:34:13.185
OK, so let's again
make our favorite code.

00:34:17.790 --> 00:34:19.739
OK.

00:34:19.739 --> 00:34:29.969
So what we will do is
we will map code words

00:34:29.969 --> 00:34:35.549
to leaves of a binary tree.

00:34:39.659 --> 00:34:42.060
So you start with the root.

00:34:42.060 --> 00:34:44.730
Every binary tree has a root.

00:34:44.730 --> 00:34:47.909
And a binary tree will--

00:34:47.909 --> 00:34:50.699
Any node in the tree
will be either a leaf,

00:34:50.699 --> 00:34:52.409
or it will have two children.

00:34:52.409 --> 00:34:55.050
So the root here
has two children.

00:34:55.050 --> 00:34:57.180
One is the left child,
which we'll call 0,

00:34:57.180 --> 00:34:59.280
and the right child is 1.

00:34:59.280 --> 00:35:04.410
And A is the leaf here.

00:35:04.410 --> 00:35:06.420
Let me draw it, and
then we will discuss.

00:35:11.140 --> 00:35:13.870
Very simple stuff really
if you think about it.

00:35:21.380 --> 00:35:25.220
So the path from
the root to the leaf

00:35:25.220 --> 00:35:30.680
gives you the code word,
basically, if we write it down.

00:35:30.680 --> 00:35:38.375
So path from root to
leaf equal to code word.

00:35:44.930 --> 00:35:48.248
Any questions?

00:35:48.248 --> 00:35:50.540
This is just a representation,
and we will briefly talk

00:35:50.540 --> 00:35:53.780
about why this is interesting.

00:35:53.780 --> 00:35:55.940
And then we will use it
in a bunch of proofs.

00:36:08.140 --> 00:36:11.350
So let me just describe you
another method of decoding

00:36:11.350 --> 00:36:12.700
that will use this tree.

00:36:27.940 --> 00:36:30.550
So the algorithm is simple.

00:36:30.550 --> 00:36:32.950
And I think you will implement--

00:36:32.950 --> 00:36:36.310
you will see the code for
this next lecture as well.

00:36:36.310 --> 00:36:38.590
So you first see a 0.

00:36:38.590 --> 00:36:40.720
So what you do is you
start at the root.

00:36:40.720 --> 00:36:43.600
You follow the 0 branch.

00:36:43.600 --> 00:36:46.060
If you reach a leaf,
you immediately decode--

00:36:46.060 --> 00:36:50.380
OK, A. Then you go
back to the root.

00:36:50.380 --> 00:36:51.730
So you reset.

00:36:51.730 --> 00:36:53.920
That's the reset step in a way.

00:36:53.920 --> 00:36:55.540
Now you're at the root again.

00:36:55.540 --> 00:36:56.770
You see a 1.

00:36:56.770 --> 00:36:58.270
You go here.

00:36:58.270 --> 00:37:01.510
You see another 1, you go again.

00:37:01.510 --> 00:37:04.450
You see a 0, you come here.

00:37:04.450 --> 00:37:08.900
You see-- oh, I hit a leaf.

00:37:08.900 --> 00:37:12.530
So I have a leaf,
so I decode, C.

00:37:12.530 --> 00:37:17.810
And then I again go back
to the root from here.

00:37:17.810 --> 00:37:20.870
Then I see a 1, I go here.

00:37:20.870 --> 00:37:24.050
I see a 0, I go here.

00:37:24.050 --> 00:37:33.642
I hit a leaf, I am
at B. I decode B.

00:37:33.642 --> 00:37:34.910
Are we happy?

00:37:34.910 --> 00:37:36.200
Should I explain again?

00:37:38.760 --> 00:37:39.330
OK.

00:37:39.330 --> 00:37:40.920
OK, we'll keep moving.

00:37:40.920 --> 00:37:46.480
And I hope you get some
practice in homeworks.

00:37:46.480 --> 00:37:48.085
You will see this several times.

00:37:54.750 --> 00:37:56.850
OK, so now a question
for everybody.

00:38:07.240 --> 00:38:08.530
Please help me.

00:38:08.530 --> 00:38:09.820
I have this other code.

00:38:09.820 --> 00:38:12.610
Please help me make a
tree out of this code.

00:38:12.610 --> 00:38:18.610
And I will make the root for
you, and I will put the A here.

00:38:18.610 --> 00:38:23.780
But can you help me
with the B and the C?

00:38:23.780 --> 00:38:24.590
Anybody?

00:38:28.414 --> 00:38:29.850
Would it be a 1?

00:38:29.850 --> 00:38:32.610
OK, so the suggestion
is to make the 1 branch.

00:38:32.610 --> 00:38:36.160
Definitely, because B and C both
start at the 1-- makes sense.

00:38:36.160 --> 00:38:38.870
Make the 0 branch.

00:38:38.870 --> 00:38:40.840
Yep, we make a 0 branch.

00:38:40.840 --> 00:38:41.965
Yep.

00:38:41.965 --> 00:38:44.830
And then you write the B.

00:38:44.830 --> 00:38:48.160
I write a B, yep.

00:38:48.160 --> 00:38:53.990
Create a 0 branch and write a C.

00:38:53.990 --> 00:38:58.190
Does this look different from
the other one in some way?

00:38:58.190 --> 00:39:00.170
Forget about the fact
that some of the nodes

00:39:00.170 --> 00:39:01.400
don't have two children.

00:39:01.400 --> 00:39:02.575
That's fine, I think.

00:39:02.575 --> 00:39:03.950
Are there any
other ways in which

00:39:03.950 --> 00:39:06.770
this differs from
the previous one?

00:39:06.770 --> 00:39:16.160
Yeah, essentially, C, it's
a child of B. Like it's--

00:39:16.160 --> 00:39:19.010
like in that one, once
you got to a letter,

00:39:19.010 --> 00:39:20.640
no children come after it.

00:39:20.640 --> 00:39:21.140
Yeah.

00:39:21.140 --> 00:39:25.460
Yeah, so the answer is basically
that here C is a child of B.

00:39:25.460 --> 00:39:30.380
So B is not really a leaf.

00:39:30.380 --> 00:39:34.390
So B is not a leaf.

00:39:34.390 --> 00:39:37.540
And the reason is that C is a--

00:39:37.540 --> 00:39:42.790
B is a prefix of C.
So B is prefix of C.

00:39:42.790 --> 00:39:45.640
You see 10 is a prefix of 100.

00:39:45.640 --> 00:39:48.910
So since B is a prefix
of C, it's not a leaf.

00:39:48.910 --> 00:39:51.070
This is not a prefix-free code.

00:39:51.070 --> 00:39:52.510
It's not a prefix--

00:39:52.510 --> 00:39:57.190
So basically, this
is a property,

00:39:57.190 --> 00:39:59.440
which, again, we'll
not prove, but very

00:39:59.440 --> 00:40:05.620
intuitive-- is
that prefix code is

00:40:05.620 --> 00:40:15.620
like equivalent to
binary tree, where

00:40:15.620 --> 00:40:17.250
all code words are leaves.

00:40:20.040 --> 00:40:22.890
And this is very obvious once
you think about it for a minute

00:40:22.890 --> 00:40:26.220
because if your code word
is not a leaf, if it's

00:40:26.220 --> 00:40:27.960
an internal node
in the tree, that

00:40:27.960 --> 00:40:31.710
means you can extend it further
and get another code word.

00:40:31.710 --> 00:40:35.700
But that means that one of
the code words is a child--

00:40:35.700 --> 00:40:38.490
like a prefix of the other.

00:40:38.490 --> 00:40:40.560
If B was a leaf, everything
would have been good.

00:40:40.560 --> 00:40:41.910
But B is not a leaf.

00:40:41.910 --> 00:40:45.450
C is a child of B. So that
means the code word for B

00:40:45.450 --> 00:40:48.360
is a prefix for
the code word of C

00:40:48.360 --> 00:40:50.175
because of the way we
define things here.

00:40:53.410 --> 00:40:55.795
Is that clear?

00:40:55.795 --> 00:40:57.670
So you see there is an
equivalence basically.

00:40:57.670 --> 00:40:59.980
Any prefix code is
equivalent to a binary tree

00:40:59.980 --> 00:41:01.525
where you have a
certain property.

00:41:07.372 --> 00:41:08.830
OK, we'll move on
to the next part,

00:41:08.830 --> 00:41:12.570
unless there are questions.

00:41:12.570 --> 00:41:15.600
OK, let's talk about
good prefix-free codes.

00:41:15.600 --> 00:41:17.807
So we now understand
uniquely decodable codes.

00:41:17.807 --> 00:41:19.140
We understand prefix-free codes.

00:41:19.140 --> 00:41:21.360
We know that prefix
codes can be represented

00:41:21.360 --> 00:41:26.760
as trees, where each of the
leaves represents a code word.

00:41:26.760 --> 00:41:29.520
And I hinted to you
but not quite proved

00:41:29.520 --> 00:41:33.090
that we will not worry about
uniquely decodable codes,

00:41:33.090 --> 00:41:36.090
because prefix-free codes are
good enough for our purposes.

00:41:36.090 --> 00:41:38.820
OK, so with that context,
let's start about,

00:41:38.820 --> 00:41:40.935
what do we mean by
good prefix-free code?

00:41:49.400 --> 00:41:51.400
So if we want to
get very formal,

00:41:51.400 --> 00:41:54.050
we'll do the proofs very
systematically in class.

00:41:54.050 --> 00:41:56.930
But just some of the other
stuff where you think we

00:41:56.930 --> 00:42:00.290
are not very rigorous,
the suggested book

00:42:00.290 --> 00:42:05.690
is Cover and Thomas
Information Theory, chapter 5.

00:42:05.690 --> 00:42:09.080
So that's where-- if you want
to read this in more detail

00:42:09.080 --> 00:42:11.540
and also a bunch of very
good questions in the--

00:42:14.070 --> 00:42:15.435
Cover was a professor here.

00:42:21.010 --> 00:42:31.970
OK, so the first property
is for symbols S1, S2.

00:42:31.970 --> 00:42:38.270
If probability of S1 is
greater than probability of S2,

00:42:38.270 --> 00:42:44.240
then the length of the code
word, l, S1, should be what?

00:42:44.240 --> 00:42:50.280
More than or-- greater or
smaller than the length for S2?

00:42:50.280 --> 00:42:50.780
Smaller.

00:42:50.780 --> 00:42:51.420
Smaller, right?

00:42:51.420 --> 00:42:54.710
Yeah, we saw this in last
class, so very intuitive

00:42:54.710 --> 00:42:57.890
from the definition of good.

00:42:57.890 --> 00:43:00.415
Good just means
that we want to--

00:43:00.415 --> 00:43:01.790
actually, I should
have defined--

00:43:01.790 --> 00:43:03.830
let me define "good,"
sorry, in the last slide.

00:43:03.830 --> 00:43:05.450
One second.

00:43:05.450 --> 00:43:13.610
Good means minimize expectation
of length of the code word

00:43:13.610 --> 00:43:18.250
subject to being a prefix code.

00:43:18.250 --> 00:43:21.010
That's like the optimization
definition of good.

00:43:25.650 --> 00:43:27.480
OK, we'll give you
a second if you--

00:43:27.480 --> 00:43:30.240
we will post these
notes as well in case

00:43:30.240 --> 00:43:31.570
you want to take a look later.

00:43:31.570 --> 00:43:34.112
But if you want to take your
own notes because you don't like

00:43:34.112 --> 00:43:36.990
my handwriting or you think
it helps you learn better,

00:43:36.990 --> 00:43:39.670
please go ahead.

00:43:39.670 --> 00:43:40.170
OK.

00:43:43.040 --> 00:43:46.190
If I had a code
where l length of S1

00:43:46.190 --> 00:43:49.202
was greater than
length of S2, can I

00:43:49.202 --> 00:43:50.660
create another code
which is just--

00:43:50.660 --> 00:43:52.790
I know which is strictly
better than my code?

00:43:55.880 --> 00:43:58.340
So the question is--

00:43:58.340 --> 00:44:08.970
this is-- if I have l
S1 greater than l S2,

00:44:08.970 --> 00:44:19.650
what I can do is I can swap
code words for S1 and S2

00:44:19.650 --> 00:44:22.860
and minimize the expected loss--

00:44:22.860 --> 00:44:24.720
expected code length.

00:44:27.510 --> 00:44:29.100
So if you give me
a bad code which

00:44:29.100 --> 00:44:31.890
has the length in the opposite
order of the probability,

00:44:31.890 --> 00:44:36.240
I could just swap them around
until increasing probability

00:44:36.240 --> 00:44:38.130
means decreasing code length.

00:44:38.130 --> 00:44:43.080
And I will just strictly get
a better expected length.

00:44:43.080 --> 00:44:45.900
So we basically
prove this property

00:44:45.900 --> 00:44:49.050
with this one line of argument.

00:44:52.710 --> 00:44:55.840
OK, let's talk about
the second property.

00:45:15.458 --> 00:45:17.000
OK, so on the second
property, I will

00:45:17.000 --> 00:45:18.710
ask you to trust us for now.

00:45:18.710 --> 00:45:20.450
We will prove it in
the next lecture.

00:45:20.450 --> 00:45:22.550
But it's very useful
to know this property,

00:45:22.550 --> 00:45:25.790
to actually learn some of the--

00:45:25.790 --> 00:45:28.610
we'll actually see an actual
code today, a good prefix

00:45:28.610 --> 00:45:31.220
code shortly, after this.

00:45:35.880 --> 00:45:38.550
The properties that
the length of a symbol

00:45:38.550 --> 00:45:45.675
x should be roughly log base
2 1 over the probability of x.

00:45:49.617 --> 00:45:52.200
All right, so this is in a way
stronger than the previous one.

00:45:52.200 --> 00:45:54.260
The previous one was a more
qualitative sort of thing,

00:45:54.260 --> 00:45:56.990
where if probability is higher,
then length should be smaller.

00:45:56.990 --> 00:45:59.360
This is just saying that
if your probability is p,

00:45:59.360 --> 00:46:02.810
then your length should
be log base 2 1 over p.

00:46:02.810 --> 00:46:05.060
This is like the
negative log likelihood

00:46:05.060 --> 00:46:07.085
term you might have
seen in other places.

00:46:12.510 --> 00:46:13.650
Let's do an example--

00:46:20.130 --> 00:46:23.160
uniform distribution, the
simplest distribution.

00:46:29.540 --> 00:46:35.870
OK, so let's say you just have A
and B with half of probability.

00:46:35.870 --> 00:46:42.020
Then log base 2 1 over 1/2
is log of 2, which is 1.

00:46:42.020 --> 00:46:43.970
So what we are
saying a good code

00:46:43.970 --> 00:46:47.330
for this distribution, where
A and B have probability 1/2

00:46:47.330 --> 00:46:50.960
each, will have length
of the code word

00:46:50.960 --> 00:46:55.070
being 1 for both A and
B. So like A going to 0,

00:46:55.070 --> 00:46:59.525
B going to 1, that's a good
code word under this criteria.

00:47:05.320 --> 00:47:11.150
OK, let me keep going and
give you another example.

00:47:11.150 --> 00:47:24.250
So in general, if x is
uniformly distributed in 1 to k,

00:47:24.250 --> 00:47:29.170
then a good code
will have length

00:47:29.170 --> 00:47:36.050
of a symbol x equal to log
base 2 k, roughly equal.

00:47:38.600 --> 00:47:43.720
Can somebody suggest when this
can be like an exact equality?

00:47:43.720 --> 00:47:46.155
When can you have length--

00:47:46.155 --> 00:47:48.250
When k is a power of 2.

00:47:48.250 --> 00:47:51.070
Yeah, so then when
k is a power of 2,

00:47:51.070 --> 00:47:52.930
you can exactly get log k.

00:47:52.930 --> 00:47:54.250
And we already saw this, right?

00:47:54.250 --> 00:47:54.880
This is nothing new.

00:47:54.880 --> 00:47:57.005
We saw the fixed length
code in the previous class.

00:47:57.005 --> 00:47:58.610
We saw it again in this class.

00:47:58.610 --> 00:47:59.770
So that's nothing new.

00:47:59.770 --> 00:48:03.145
So this is just the fixed length
code that we know and love.

00:48:07.910 --> 00:48:12.110
But in general, the best you
can do with fixed length code

00:48:12.110 --> 00:48:14.690
is lx equal to the
ceiling of log k.

00:48:14.690 --> 00:48:17.090
You can't quite do like--

00:48:17.090 --> 00:48:20.270
because it might not
be an integer, the log.

00:48:20.270 --> 00:48:22.340
So already, you see
some justification

00:48:22.340 --> 00:48:23.390
for this property, right?

00:48:23.390 --> 00:48:27.350
For our fixed length code,
you can actually achieve it.

00:48:27.350 --> 00:48:29.570
And you also see that for
the uniform distribution,

00:48:29.570 --> 00:48:30.440
it's a good code.

00:48:30.440 --> 00:48:32.300
In general, it need
not be a good code.

00:48:32.300 --> 00:48:34.700
But for the uniform
distribution, which also

00:48:34.700 --> 00:48:36.050
intuitively makes sense where--

00:48:36.050 --> 00:48:38.900
since all of the symbols
have the same probability,

00:48:38.900 --> 00:48:41.960
you want them to have the same
length, the code word length.

00:48:41.960 --> 00:48:44.120
And the code word length
must be this thing.

00:48:46.760 --> 00:48:50.450
OK, please raise your
hand with any questions.

00:48:50.450 --> 00:48:51.530
Let's do another example.

00:48:54.230 --> 00:48:58.820
Let's look at our
favorite code that we

00:48:58.820 --> 00:49:12.150
have been looking at for a
bit, the 0, 10, 110, 111.

00:49:12.150 --> 00:49:15.040
You will notice that we
don't have a length--

00:49:15.040 --> 00:49:17.010
sorry, we don't have a
probability distribution

00:49:17.010 --> 00:49:18.450
for this at the moment.

00:49:18.450 --> 00:49:23.290
Let me define a probability
distribution, 1/2, 1/4, 1/8,

00:49:23.290 --> 00:49:23.790
1/8.

00:49:27.360 --> 00:49:32.970
Let me also create
the log of 1 over px.

00:49:32.970 --> 00:49:37.700
So it will be 1, 2, 3, 3.

00:49:37.700 --> 00:49:41.720
So as you might notice,
for this particular code

00:49:41.720 --> 00:49:44.610
and for this particular
distribution,

00:49:44.610 --> 00:49:47.610
the length of the code word
exactly matches the log 1

00:49:47.610 --> 00:49:49.710
by probability, 0.

00:49:49.710 --> 00:49:51.660
The length here
is 1 the log is 1.

00:49:51.660 --> 00:49:53.670
The length for the C and D is 3.

00:49:53.670 --> 00:49:54.840
The log is 3.

00:49:58.030 --> 00:50:02.080
And this we will actually
see not today but Wednesday

00:50:02.080 --> 00:50:05.980
that this code is the optimal
code for this probability

00:50:05.980 --> 00:50:07.920
distribution.

00:50:07.920 --> 00:50:09.880
We are getting closer
and closer to making

00:50:09.880 --> 00:50:13.300
very powerful statements about
codes in general, which we'll

00:50:13.300 --> 00:50:14.890
use throughout the course.

00:50:20.540 --> 00:50:24.645
OK, for now, I think
that's it I'm going to say.

00:50:24.645 --> 00:50:26.270
Just remember there
are two properties,

00:50:26.270 --> 00:50:29.660
where really the second property
[AUDIO OUT] first property.

00:50:29.660 --> 00:50:34.100
So focus on the second property,
that the length of your code

00:50:34.100 --> 00:50:36.830
word should be roughly
equal to the log of 1

00:50:36.830 --> 00:50:38.030
over the probability.

00:50:40.980 --> 00:50:41.970
Any questions on this?

00:50:41.970 --> 00:50:45.000
Because after this, we're going
to see a code construction that

00:50:45.000 --> 00:50:46.260
will actually achieve this.

00:50:50.260 --> 00:50:51.520
OK, [INAUDIBLE].

00:50:51.520 --> 00:50:52.660
Good.

00:50:52.660 --> 00:50:56.872
OK, so we will now
see Shannon code.

00:50:56.872 --> 00:50:57.580
I don't know if--

00:50:57.580 --> 00:50:59.920
I think Pulkit mentioned
Shannon in the last--

00:50:59.920 --> 00:51:01.000
the intro lecture.

00:51:01.000 --> 00:51:04.900
So Claude Shannon, he did
a lot of great things.

00:51:04.900 --> 00:51:06.580
Among them, he
basically invented

00:51:06.580 --> 00:51:09.430
the field of information
theory, which has two big parts.

00:51:09.430 --> 00:51:12.010
One is compression, which
we will learn in this class.

00:51:12.010 --> 00:51:13.720
And the other is error
correction coding,

00:51:13.720 --> 00:51:15.730
which is also very,
very important,

00:51:15.730 --> 00:51:18.880
used in every
communication you do.

00:51:18.880 --> 00:51:21.940
So this is one of
the cases where

00:51:21.940 --> 00:51:26.170
Shannon himself created
this particular code, which

00:51:26.170 --> 00:51:31.090
is a good prefix-free code,
according to the definition,

00:51:31.090 --> 00:51:32.500
the properties we saw.

00:51:37.880 --> 00:51:40.580
I guess I won't say it
right now, but maybe as we

00:51:40.580 --> 00:51:42.590
go through the first
half of the course,

00:51:42.590 --> 00:51:45.710
if you get your hands
on the 1948 paper

00:51:45.710 --> 00:51:48.080
by Shannon, which is
called "A Mathematical

00:51:48.080 --> 00:51:51.140
Theory of Communication,"
read the first part, the part

00:51:51.140 --> 00:51:52.970
that covers compression.

00:51:52.970 --> 00:51:54.920
It's very interesting,
written in very simple

00:51:54.920 --> 00:51:59.870
words, not very fancy math
stuff-- very, very simple

00:51:59.870 --> 00:52:02.690
words, very intuitive.

00:52:02.690 --> 00:52:05.640
You might find it interesting.

00:52:05.640 --> 00:52:07.445
OK, so Shannon code.

00:52:23.880 --> 00:52:26.205
So this will be a prefix code.

00:52:30.050 --> 00:52:36.770
And for symbol x,
we will actually

00:52:36.770 --> 00:52:41.780
achieve the length of the code
word, will be log base 2 1

00:52:41.780 --> 00:52:44.870
over probability of x.

00:52:44.870 --> 00:52:47.930
You might have even seen this
log base 2 last lecture, also

00:52:47.930 --> 00:52:49.970
this lecture as well.

00:52:49.970 --> 00:52:52.400
Does anybody know
why we see this 2?

00:52:52.400 --> 00:52:54.590
Why not log base
3 or natural log

00:52:54.590 --> 00:52:57.058
or log base 10 or
something like that?

00:52:57.058 --> 00:52:58.600
Because if we're
dealing with binary,

00:52:58.600 --> 00:53:01.190
it's just 0's and 1's,
and it's encoding to bits.

00:53:01.190 --> 00:53:02.172
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:53:02.172 --> 00:53:04.130
So the answer is, if we
are dealing with binary

00:53:04.130 --> 00:53:06.463
because our output is binary,
we are encoding everything

00:53:06.463 --> 00:53:07.700
into bits.

00:53:07.700 --> 00:53:10.790
In another world if
we had semiconductors

00:53:10.790 --> 00:53:12.500
that are like three
levels or something,

00:53:12.500 --> 00:53:15.680
you could be working
with log base 3.

00:53:15.680 --> 00:53:17.570
Yeah, so you see
it's a prefix code.

00:53:17.570 --> 00:53:21.170
And for any symbol x, it will
achieve log 1 over p of x.

00:53:21.170 --> 00:53:24.170
So it's very good
code in that regard.

00:53:40.730 --> 00:53:42.770
OK, let's do the construction.

00:53:51.100 --> 00:53:55.720
Step one, you compute the lx,
which is this function we just

00:53:55.720 --> 00:53:59.550
saw, the log 1 over px.

00:53:59.550 --> 00:54:01.920
In this class, if you
see a log and somewhere

00:54:01.920 --> 00:54:04.590
we forget to put
the 2, just assume

00:54:04.590 --> 00:54:07.125
it's log base 2, unless
otherwise stated.

00:54:10.220 --> 00:54:17.905
For each x, you
compute it, sort in--

00:54:23.530 --> 00:54:32.960
sort symbols in lx1 greater than
or equal to lx2 dot dot dot,

00:54:32.960 --> 00:54:36.620
increasing order of the lengths.

00:54:36.620 --> 00:54:44.340
For each symbol x,
so in the above order

00:54:44.340 --> 00:54:46.800
that we just sorted them in--

00:54:53.860 --> 00:54:55.600
it's a very, very simple--

00:54:55.600 --> 00:54:59.320
if you have done algorithms,
this is a greedy algorithm.

00:54:59.320 --> 00:55:04.780
Assign leaf of
the-- a prefix code

00:55:04.780 --> 00:55:06.190
is a tree, as you already saw.

00:55:06.190 --> 00:55:20.490
So you're just going to
assign a tree at depth lx

00:55:20.490 --> 00:55:30.690
without violating
prefix condition.

00:55:35.440 --> 00:55:36.940
And this, I think,
you will actually

00:55:36.940 --> 00:55:39.310
implement in the first homework.

00:55:39.310 --> 00:55:40.960
Very simple idea.

00:55:40.960 --> 00:55:42.940
What you do is--

00:55:42.940 --> 00:55:47.200
you know that your lengths have
to be this log 1 by px thing,

00:55:47.200 --> 00:55:47.830
right?

00:55:47.830 --> 00:55:49.600
So you already
compute the lengths.

00:55:49.600 --> 00:55:53.050
You saw the symbols in the order
of their increasing length.

00:55:53.050 --> 00:55:55.420
Then, for each symbol,
let's say I say

00:55:55.420 --> 00:55:56.920
a symbol which has length 1.

00:55:56.920 --> 00:55:59.930
So I [AUDIO OUT] the tree--

00:55:59.930 --> 00:56:03.030
which leaves of length 1 are
available without violating

00:56:03.030 --> 00:56:03.530
the prefix?

00:56:03.530 --> 00:56:05.000
And we'll see an example.

00:56:05.000 --> 00:56:06.980
You assign that greedily.

00:56:06.980 --> 00:56:08.630
You don't need to
think too much.

00:56:08.630 --> 00:56:12.590
Next symbol you
see maybe length 3.

00:56:12.590 --> 00:56:14.810
You find a place
in the tree which

00:56:14.810 --> 00:56:19.460
has depth 3, which if it is not
already a child of something

00:56:19.460 --> 00:56:20.750
else, and you assign it.

00:56:20.750 --> 00:56:21.740
And you go on, go on.

00:56:21.740 --> 00:56:25.280
And then you have a code
which will work out.

00:56:27.980 --> 00:56:28.970
Let's do an example.

00:56:28.970 --> 00:56:30.830
And then if anybody
has questions,

00:56:30.830 --> 00:56:33.680
we will go through
the questions.

00:56:33.680 --> 00:56:34.940
I think the example will help.

00:56:39.260 --> 00:56:43.252
So A, B, C. We'll take a
slightly bigger example

00:56:43.252 --> 00:56:44.085
so that it's easier.

00:56:54.630 --> 00:56:56.670
So this is x, the symbol.

00:56:56.670 --> 00:56:59.430
px is the probability
of the symbol.

00:56:59.430 --> 00:57:02.130
And let's compute l
of x, which I computed

00:57:02.130 --> 00:57:04.620
at home to save us time.

00:57:04.620 --> 00:57:08.335
So 2, 2, 3, 3, 5.

00:57:08.335 --> 00:57:08.835
OK.

00:57:12.990 --> 00:57:14.580
And then as we
work through it, we

00:57:14.580 --> 00:57:15.960
will assign these code words.

00:57:15.960 --> 00:57:19.430
But when we start, we don't
have anything to go with.

00:57:24.420 --> 00:57:28.457
OK, so I start.

00:57:28.457 --> 00:57:29.790
I have nothing initially, right?

00:57:29.790 --> 00:57:32.460
They're already sorted
in the increasing length

00:57:32.460 --> 00:57:33.870
so that that's also done.

00:57:33.870 --> 00:57:35.220
So step one is already done.

00:57:35.220 --> 00:57:36.360
Step two is done.

00:57:36.360 --> 00:57:40.140
Now, the first symbol
you see is A. Can

00:57:40.140 --> 00:57:41.850
you suggest any code
word of length 2

00:57:41.850 --> 00:57:46.202
that I can assign without
violating the prefix property?

00:57:46.202 --> 00:57:48.130
00.

00:57:48.130 --> 00:57:49.510
Yeah, so one answer is 00.

00:57:49.510 --> 00:57:52.180
I could have done
11, 10, all four.

00:57:52.180 --> 00:57:54.940
Right now, there is no code,
so there is no prefix property.

00:57:54.940 --> 00:57:57.040
So anything is fine.

00:57:57.040 --> 00:57:59.245
So let's assign 00 to
keep things simple.

00:58:02.080 --> 00:58:06.960
So I've assigned 00
to A at this point.

00:58:06.960 --> 00:58:10.080
OK, now for B. B,
I need to assign

00:58:10.080 --> 00:58:12.840
again a length to a code word
without violating the prefix

00:58:12.840 --> 00:58:14.700
property.

00:58:14.700 --> 00:58:17.060
Suggestions?

00:58:17.060 --> 00:58:18.341
01.

00:58:18.341 --> 00:58:19.970
01, OK, I like it.

00:58:19.970 --> 00:58:22.340
I like people that
are not straying off

00:58:22.340 --> 00:58:24.765
the code that I made at home.

00:58:24.765 --> 00:58:25.265
OK.

00:58:27.840 --> 00:58:30.465
OK, for C, I need a length 3.

00:58:33.500 --> 00:58:35.060
What about 000?

00:58:35.060 --> 00:58:35.735
Is that good?

00:58:38.240 --> 00:58:39.380
000 is not good.

00:58:39.380 --> 00:58:43.970
It's a child of A, so it will
violate the prefix property.

00:58:43.970 --> 00:58:47.530
So let me assign something.

00:58:47.530 --> 00:58:52.120
I need to go to this
branch, I think.

00:58:52.120 --> 00:58:58.130
Let's just take this path, 111.

00:58:58.130 --> 00:59:01.310
When you actually implement
it, what you would want to do

00:59:01.310 --> 00:59:03.703
is not to take any random node.

00:59:03.703 --> 00:59:05.870
What you would want is sort
of go lexicographically.

00:59:05.870 --> 00:59:08.390
It just makes everything
easier, makes your algorithm

00:59:08.390 --> 00:59:09.410
more deterministic.

00:59:09.410 --> 00:59:12.590
But for illustration purpose,
you can choose anything.

00:59:12.590 --> 00:59:13.790
It's fine.

00:59:13.790 --> 00:59:19.325
So I assigned 111 to C. Now
to D, any suggestions for D?

00:59:24.730 --> 00:59:26.570
110?

00:59:26.570 --> 00:59:29.170
110, but let me not do that.

00:59:29.170 --> 00:59:32.260
Let me do 101.

00:59:32.260 --> 00:59:35.830
So 101, I will do D, just to
show that it doesn't really

00:59:35.830 --> 00:59:36.340
matter.

00:59:36.340 --> 00:59:40.240
There are a bunch of
possibilities here.

00:59:40.240 --> 00:59:45.000
OK, last one, E. E you
need a length 5 code word.

00:59:49.230 --> 00:59:51.270
so let me do it.

00:59:51.270 --> 00:59:56.985
Let me take a path from here
and then here and then here.

01:00:07.710 --> 01:00:09.330
OK, so we got a
prefix-free code.

01:00:09.330 --> 01:00:11.130
It satisfies this
length condition.

01:00:11.130 --> 01:00:14.610
Everything is good.

01:00:14.610 --> 01:00:17.225
Question, is this a good code?

01:00:17.225 --> 01:00:18.600
Do you think it's
the best code I

01:00:18.600 --> 01:00:20.460
can make for this distribution?

01:00:23.950 --> 01:00:24.880
OK, let's do a vote.

01:00:24.880 --> 01:00:28.240
How many of you think this is
the absolute best code for this

01:00:28.240 --> 01:00:32.410
distribution, you cannot
beat it in any way in terms

01:00:32.410 --> 01:00:35.050
of the expected code length?

01:00:35.050 --> 01:00:39.350
How many of you think it is
not the absolute best code?

01:00:39.350 --> 01:00:41.090
OK, so I see a bunch
of raised hands

01:00:41.090 --> 01:00:43.610
for not the absolute best.

01:00:43.610 --> 01:00:47.630
One of you, why do you think
it's not the absolute best

01:00:47.630 --> 01:00:49.910
code for this distribution?

01:00:49.910 --> 01:00:52.610
E could be significantly
smaller length.

01:00:52.610 --> 01:00:53.150
Yeah.

01:00:53.150 --> 01:00:55.880
Yeah, so the answer
is E could be smaller.

01:00:55.880 --> 01:01:00.350
We are-- what's happening here,
this is not something we like.

01:01:00.350 --> 01:01:01.520
You have E here.

01:01:01.520 --> 01:01:04.257
You could have placed
E here without--

01:01:04.257 --> 01:01:08.570
sorry, I don't know how to undo.

01:01:08.570 --> 01:01:11.570
Let me--

01:01:11.570 --> 01:01:14.030
Yeah, so you could have
made E much smaller

01:01:14.030 --> 01:01:16.802
without affecting the
prefix-free property.

01:01:16.802 --> 01:01:18.260
And hence, you
could have improved.

01:01:18.260 --> 01:01:21.020
Even C, C is not very good.

01:01:21.020 --> 01:01:23.960
C you could have placed here.

01:01:23.960 --> 01:01:26.280
So we said that Shannon
codes are good codes.

01:01:26.280 --> 01:01:28.250
So they are good in
a sense that we will

01:01:28.250 --> 01:01:30.320
define in the next lecture.

01:01:30.320 --> 01:01:32.450
They come very close
to the optimal,

01:01:32.450 --> 01:01:34.760
but they are not
necessarily optimal.

01:01:34.760 --> 01:01:39.080
Next Monday, we will learn
about the code which is optimal.

01:01:39.080 --> 01:01:42.320
But for now, I guess we will
live with what we have got.

01:01:42.320 --> 01:01:43.520
And this is the code.

01:01:56.860 --> 01:02:02.180
I'll show that
this construction--

01:02:02.180 --> 01:02:05.180
so if you like-- if you read
this construction carefully,

01:02:05.180 --> 01:02:07.400
what you will observe
is step one is obvious.

01:02:07.400 --> 01:02:08.930
There is nothing there.

01:02:08.930 --> 01:02:10.220
Step two is obvious.

01:02:10.220 --> 01:02:11.750
You can always sort things.

01:02:11.750 --> 01:02:13.910
Step three depends
on the fact that when

01:02:13.910 --> 01:02:15.770
you are trying to
assign a leaf, there

01:02:15.770 --> 01:02:17.180
should be a leaf available.

01:02:17.180 --> 01:02:21.380
If there is no leaf available,
then you can't assign anything.

01:02:21.380 --> 01:02:26.320
So actually, if you have
done the greedy algorithms,

01:02:26.320 --> 01:02:28.930
if you have done the minimum
spanning tree or something,

01:02:28.930 --> 01:02:33.580
you always need to prove that
the next greedy step is indeed

01:02:33.580 --> 01:02:36.130
possible.

01:02:36.130 --> 01:02:39.325
So really, the thing to prove
here, which we'll prove now--

01:02:42.700 --> 01:02:44.260
it's like an inductive proof.

01:02:44.260 --> 01:02:45.550
So we need to--

01:02:45.550 --> 01:02:47.990
we need to prove that this
construction always works.

01:02:47.990 --> 01:03:02.130
So we need to prove a leaf at
lx depth is always available--

01:03:09.790 --> 01:03:16.460
always available without
violating the prefix condition.

01:03:23.440 --> 01:03:28.990
OK, so let me go
through the proof.

01:03:28.990 --> 01:03:33.590
And stop me if you
have questions.

01:03:36.210 --> 01:03:40.650
If you have not done proofs
before, don't worry too much.

01:03:40.650 --> 01:03:43.770
Only very small part of
the course hinges on this.

01:03:43.770 --> 01:03:46.110
I hope you find the
proof intuitive.

01:03:46.110 --> 01:03:47.525
It's not like one
of those proofs

01:03:47.525 --> 01:03:48.900
where suddenly
you get the result

01:03:48.900 --> 01:03:50.700
and you have no
idea what happened.

01:03:50.700 --> 01:03:53.880
It's very obvious in some ways.

01:03:53.880 --> 01:03:57.480
OK, so proof.

01:04:02.110 --> 01:04:06.560
So if we take all the things--

01:04:06.560 --> 01:04:09.280
we take the sum--

01:04:09.280 --> 01:04:14.350
this sum is 2 power-- we know
that the length is log base 2 1

01:04:14.350 --> 01:04:16.180
over p of x.

01:04:16.180 --> 01:04:19.120
If you've forgotten
the properties of logs,

01:04:19.120 --> 01:04:20.530
now is a good time to refresh.

01:04:23.780 --> 01:04:26.090
Now, you know that the
ceiling of something

01:04:26.090 --> 01:04:28.040
is bigger than the original.

01:04:28.040 --> 01:04:30.770
Ceiling of x is bigger
than or equal to--

01:04:30.770 --> 01:04:32.480
bigger than or equal to x.

01:04:32.480 --> 01:04:36.680
So minus ceiling of x will be
smaller than or equal to x.

01:04:36.680 --> 01:04:38.930
So after a bunch of--

01:04:38.930 --> 01:04:42.770
after like two steps, basically,
you can convert this 2.

01:04:42.770 --> 01:04:44.460
You can remove the
ceiling function,

01:04:44.460 --> 01:04:47.350
which just causes problems.

01:04:47.350 --> 01:04:50.050
And then just properties
of logs, right?

01:04:50.050 --> 01:04:55.180
2 power minus log 1 over p of x
is just-- this whole thing is--

01:04:55.180 --> 01:04:59.200
this whole thing is just p of x.

01:04:59.200 --> 01:05:04.420
So this is summation p of x.

01:05:04.420 --> 01:05:06.070
And this I think
all of you know.

01:05:06.070 --> 01:05:08.320
What is the sum
of probabilities?

01:05:10.860 --> 01:05:11.370
1.

01:05:11.370 --> 01:05:12.360
Yes, I'm seeing 1.

01:05:12.360 --> 01:05:14.520
OK, 1, right?

01:05:14.520 --> 01:05:22.710
So let me note this down as
point one, 1 power minus x

01:05:22.710 --> 01:05:25.230
less than or equal to 1.

01:05:25.230 --> 01:05:26.610
This is one.

01:05:30.130 --> 01:05:33.655
OK, let's keep going.

01:05:38.060 --> 01:05:44.270
OK, so say we assigned--

01:05:44.270 --> 01:05:45.740
so this is an inductive proof.

01:05:45.740 --> 01:05:47.810
So you say that you have
already done m steps,

01:05:47.810 --> 01:05:50.900
and now you will prove that
the m plus 1 step is possible.

01:05:50.900 --> 01:05:54.960
So we assigned x1 to xm.

01:05:54.960 --> 01:05:59.680
So next is xm plus 1.

01:05:59.680 --> 01:06:05.790
So then we already
saw above that the sum

01:06:05.790 --> 01:06:08.670
of all the probabilities
is less than or equal to 1.

01:06:08.670 --> 01:06:13.290
So therefore, if
you add from 1 to m,

01:06:13.290 --> 01:06:15.120
and then you add
the next one, this

01:06:15.120 --> 01:06:17.970
is clearly smaller
than the total sum.

01:06:17.970 --> 01:06:21.620
So this will be less than 1.

01:06:21.620 --> 01:06:29.940
So this is based on one
because this is a partial sum.

01:06:29.940 --> 01:06:32.190
This is only part
of the total sum.

01:06:35.820 --> 01:06:39.120
This one was over all
symbols in your alphabet.

01:06:39.120 --> 01:06:41.880
We are now just restricting
to first m plus 1 symbols.

01:06:46.610 --> 01:06:51.140
Or-- So what I will do
now is sort of the key.

01:06:51.140 --> 01:06:55.730
I will multiply both sides
by 2 power l xm plus 1.

01:06:55.730 --> 01:06:59.720
And then I will move one of
the terms to the right to get--

01:07:17.330 --> 01:07:18.215
minus 1.

01:07:25.060 --> 01:07:29.440
OK, until now, I was
just multiplying things,

01:07:29.440 --> 01:07:32.428
moving around things, no
special logic until now.

01:07:32.428 --> 01:07:33.970
So let me move this
to the next page,

01:07:33.970 --> 01:07:39.320
and then we will look
at some actual logic.

01:07:39.320 --> 01:07:43.700
OK, so I will copy over
the last point again.

01:07:43.700 --> 01:07:53.800
So i equal to 1 to m, 2 power l
xm plus 1 minus l xi less than

01:07:53.800 --> 01:07:57.990
or equal to 2 power
xm plus 1 minus 1.

01:08:01.300 --> 01:08:02.560
Let's call it two.

01:08:08.320 --> 01:08:14.870
Now, this is the part where
a visual representation is

01:08:14.870 --> 01:08:16.550
much, much easier to interpret.

01:08:16.550 --> 01:08:19.609
So let's look at this.

01:08:19.609 --> 01:08:24.470
If you have a tree,
and they are at depth--

01:08:27.270 --> 01:08:33.680
let's say you are at depth li,
you have a node at depth li,

01:08:33.680 --> 01:08:39.399
and you have a subtree
below this with total depth

01:08:39.399 --> 01:08:40.945
lm plus 1.

01:08:44.950 --> 01:08:48.670
OK, so first, simple question.

01:08:48.670 --> 01:08:53.709
How many nodes do I have at
depth l for a binary tree?

01:08:57.540 --> 01:08:58.510
2 to the l, right?

01:08:58.510 --> 01:09:03.250
So in a binary tree at depth
l, I have 2 to the l nodes.

01:09:03.250 --> 01:09:11.550
Now, if I have this node at li
and then I create a subtree,

01:09:11.550 --> 01:09:15.680
a tree below that node,
with overall depth l plus 1,

01:09:15.680 --> 01:09:17.359
how many nodes do I have here?

01:09:17.359 --> 01:09:19.880
How many nodes do I have here?

01:09:19.880 --> 01:09:23.300
How many nodes of
depth l plus 1 are

01:09:23.300 --> 01:09:25.490
children of a node
or descendants

01:09:25.490 --> 01:09:27.229
of a node at depth li?

01:09:30.760 --> 01:09:34.109
2 to the lm plus 1 minus li.

01:09:34.109 --> 01:09:38.840
Yes, so let me write that down.

01:09:38.840 --> 01:09:48.700
So number of leaves
at lm plus 1 that

01:09:48.700 --> 01:09:58.750
are descendants of
depth li node are

01:09:58.750 --> 01:10:02.465
2 to the lm plus 1 minus li.

01:10:02.465 --> 01:10:02.965
OK.

01:10:08.480 --> 01:10:11.060
Now, let's come back
to this inequality.

01:10:11.060 --> 01:10:13.670
What is it saying really?

01:10:13.670 --> 01:10:15.800
Each of these terms,
what is it saying?

01:10:19.780 --> 01:10:31.390
So this term, this is
number of descendants

01:10:31.390 --> 01:10:37.330
of the code word of xi,
basically, that leaf,

01:10:37.330 --> 01:10:44.320
at depth l xm plus 1.

01:10:44.320 --> 01:10:46.720
What is this term?

01:10:46.720 --> 01:10:57.085
This term is the total number
of leaves at l xm plus 1.

01:11:00.490 --> 01:11:03.670
So what we are saying is if
you take all the nodes you have

01:11:03.670 --> 01:11:07.060
already added, like all the
leafs you have already added,

01:11:07.060 --> 01:11:12.170
you extend each of them
to depth l xm plus 1,

01:11:12.170 --> 01:11:17.580
you see that there is still
at least one node left.

01:11:17.580 --> 01:11:20.610
And let me draw it for you.

01:11:20.610 --> 01:11:23.760
So what we are saying, and
please think about this at home

01:11:23.760 --> 01:11:26.905
as well, is--

01:11:35.850 --> 01:11:40.770
so what we did was--

01:11:40.770 --> 01:11:45.990
so you assigned x1 here, you
assigned x2 here, x three here

01:11:45.990 --> 01:11:47.550
maybe you assigned x4 here.

01:11:47.550 --> 01:11:48.938
You are now at x5.

01:11:48.938 --> 01:11:49.980
x5 is at a certain depth.

01:11:49.980 --> 01:11:54.340
So this is like your l x5.

01:11:54.340 --> 01:11:57.810
Now we are saying, let's
take each of these guys,

01:11:57.810 --> 01:12:01.605
extend them to the level right
here, like this, this, this.

01:12:06.260 --> 01:12:09.900
So all of these
areas are invalid.

01:12:09.900 --> 01:12:10.400
Why?

01:12:10.400 --> 01:12:16.190
Because any node
here will be a child,

01:12:16.190 --> 01:12:18.770
a descendant of
the x1 node so x1

01:12:18.770 --> 01:12:20.810
will be a prefix if you
try to assign it here.

01:12:20.810 --> 01:12:21.800
That's not good.

01:12:21.800 --> 01:12:24.530
Similarly, any node
in this area is bad.

01:12:24.530 --> 01:12:28.890
So for example, if x1 was 00,
these nodes will be like 0011,

01:12:28.890 --> 01:12:31.610
0000, all of these guys.

01:12:31.610 --> 01:12:33.980
So these are just not allowed
these are not allowed.

01:12:33.980 --> 01:12:34.610
These are not allowed.

01:12:34.610 --> 01:12:35.570
These are not allowed.

01:12:35.570 --> 01:12:37.153
So what we are trying
is we are trying

01:12:37.153 --> 01:12:39.110
to eliminate all the
nodes that are disallowed

01:12:39.110 --> 01:12:40.880
by the prefix-free property.

01:12:40.880 --> 01:12:46.640
And what we are showing is
that even after you remove

01:12:46.640 --> 01:12:49.430
every node that is illegal,
like which is not allowed,

01:12:49.430 --> 01:12:53.285
you are still left with one
node, which is still valid.

01:12:56.610 --> 01:12:57.110
Right.

01:12:57.110 --> 01:12:58.310
Any questions on this?

01:13:02.720 --> 01:13:04.470
I will give you time
to digest, I guess.

01:13:04.470 --> 01:13:06.450
This is also written
up in the notes, which

01:13:06.450 --> 01:13:07.658
are available on the website.

01:13:19.410 --> 01:13:20.400
OK, yeah, yeah.

01:13:31.320 --> 01:13:34.605
So just to finish
off the proof--

01:13:41.460 --> 01:13:45.820
so if you just
write it like this,

01:13:45.820 --> 01:13:52.830
this is the total
number of leaves at l xm

01:13:52.830 --> 01:14:01.060
plus 1 depth minus
summation 1 to m.

01:14:09.390 --> 01:14:19.620
So this is the number
of leaves not allowed

01:14:19.620 --> 01:14:34.050
because they are descended
from existing code word.

01:14:34.050 --> 01:14:36.250
So what we have basically
shown is that this

01:14:36.250 --> 01:14:37.500
is greater than or equal to 1.

01:14:37.500 --> 01:14:39.450
So there is at
least one leaf left

01:14:39.450 --> 01:14:43.020
at every step which you can
assign to your next symbol,

01:14:43.020 --> 01:14:44.220
to xm plus 1.

01:14:44.220 --> 01:14:47.310
So at depth l xm plus 1,
there is at least one leaf

01:14:47.310 --> 01:14:48.600
which is left.

01:14:48.600 --> 01:14:51.375
So that is basically the proof.

01:14:55.930 --> 01:14:57.510
Sorry if it was too much.

01:14:57.510 --> 01:15:00.763
But I guess you have to see it.

01:15:00.763 --> 01:15:03.180
Next class, we'll see another
proof which is very similar,

01:15:03.180 --> 01:15:04.263
so I think that will help.

01:15:13.500 --> 01:15:15.360
And we will put these up.

01:15:52.480 --> 01:15:54.760
Right.

01:15:54.760 --> 01:15:56.380
I think I will not
have time to cover

01:15:56.380 --> 01:15:58.960
the last topic, Kraft's
inequality, which

01:15:58.960 --> 01:16:01.390
we'll do next lecture.

01:16:01.390 --> 01:16:04.780
I will leave it here and
answer any questions.

01:16:04.780 --> 01:16:06.340
Otherwise, thank you.

01:16:09.735 --> 01:16:11.110
So don't be scared
of the proofs,

01:16:11.110 --> 01:16:14.860
if you are scared, because
they're only for two lectures.

01:16:14.860 --> 01:16:17.620
Then it's mostly algorithmic.

01:16:17.620 --> 01:16:20.470
And if you're scared
about it, make examples.

01:16:20.470 --> 01:16:22.300
I think that will really help.

01:16:22.300 --> 01:16:26.680
Just make some trees and
see what these quantities

01:16:26.680 --> 01:16:29.230
are at various steps.

01:16:29.230 --> 01:16:31.050
Thank you.

